select energy_manager.load_cards($cards$[
{"id":"p2-b07-041","paper":2,"topic":"Steam Economics","kind":"problem","front":"A back-pressure turbine plant runs round the clock: steam 40 TPH, electrical output 3,200 kW. The process needs 44 TPH, which the existing boiler can supply through the turbine. With the same steam-to-power ratio and grid electricity at Rs 7/kWh, what is the daily saving from extra generation?","back":"Rs 53,760 per day (320 kW extra x 24 h x Rs 7/kWh).","explain":"The extra steam generates extra power at the existing steam-to-power ratio. Auxiliary power is unchanged, so the whole extra power displaces grid purchases.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 07 p242","steps":[{"prompt":"Present steam-to-power recovery ratio.","answer":"40,000 kg/h / 3,200 kWh = 12.5 kg/kWh.","explain":"This is the steam needed per unit of power in the existing machine. It is assumed to stay the same."},{"prompt":"Additional steam.","answer":"44 TPH - 40 TPH = 4 TPH = 4,000 kg/h.","explain":"The extra steam the existing boiler can supply."},{"prompt":"Additional power potential.","answer":"4,000 / 12.5 = 320 kW.","explain":"Divide the extra steam by the steam-to-power ratio."},{"prompt":"Daily saving.","answer":"320 kW x 24 h x Rs 7/kWh = Rs 53,760 per day.","explain":"Extra generated power offsets grid electricity bought at Rs 7/kWh over 24 hours."}],"order":2377},
{"id":"p2-b07-042","paper":2,"topic":"Steam Economics","kind":"problem","front":"In the same plant (boiler efficiency 80%, coal GCV 4,300 kcal/kg, steam enthalpy 780 kcal/kg, feed water at 135 °C), how much additional coal is needed per day for the extra 4 TPH of steam?","back":"750 kg/h of coal, which is 18,000 kg (18 tonnes) per day.","explain":"The coal calculation uses the enthalpy rise from feed water to steam, divided by boiler efficiency and calorific value. It is the cost side of the extra power.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 07 p242","steps":[{"prompt":"Heat added per kg of steam.","answer":"780 - 135 = 645 kcal/kg.","explain":"The enthalpy rise from feed water at 135 °C to steam at 780 kcal/kg."},{"prompt":"Heat for the extra steam.","answer":"4,000 kg/h x 645 = 2,580,000 kcal/h.","explain":"Total heat load of the additional 4 TPH."},{"prompt":"Additional coal per hour.","answer":"2,580,000 / (0.80 x 4,300) = 750 kg/h.","explain":"Divide by boiler efficiency and the coal's GCV."},{"prompt":"Additional coal per day.","answer":"750 kg/h x 24 h = 18,000 kg = 18 tonnes/day.","explain":"The plant runs round the clock, so the hourly figure is multiplied by 24."}],"order":2378},
{"id":"p2-b08-001","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"What is waste heat, and what matters more than its amount in a recovery study?","back":"Waste heat is heat generated by fuel combustion or a chemical reaction that is dumped into the environment although it could still be reused economically. The essential quality of heat is its 'value', not its amount.","explain":"The strategy for recovering heat depends partly on the temperature of the waste gases and on the economics involved. Two streams with the same heat content can have very different value, so temperature decides how practical recovery is.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p247","order":2379},
{"id":"p2-b08-002","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"Why is recovering heat from a high-temperature waste stream usually more cost-effective?","back":"Higher temperature means higher quality heat, so recovery is usually more cost-effective.","explain":"Heat at a higher temperature has more potential value for a useful purpose. Low-temperature waste heat generally needs a use that can accept it directly, such as space heating.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p247","order":2380},
{"id":"p2-b08-003","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"What must exist before any waste heat recovery study is worthwhile?","back":"There must be some use for the recovered heat, such as preheating combustion air, space heating, or preheating boiler feed water or process water.","explain":"The book stresses that recovered heat with no use is not a saving. Identify the sink for the heat before selecting any equipment.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p247","order":2381},
{"id":"p2-b08-004","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"What is a cascade system of waste heat recovery?","back":"Recovery in stages: the high-temperature stage is used first (for example for air preheating), and the lower-temperature stage is used for process feed water heating or steam raising.","explain":"Cascading matches each temperature level with a suitable use. This ensures the maximum amount of heat is recovered at the highest potential.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p247","order":2382},
{"id":"p2-b08-005","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"problem","front":"Flue gas from a heat treatment furnace leaves at 900 °C at 2,100 m³/h. It can be cooled to 180 °C. With density 1.19 kg/m³ and Cp 0.24 kcal/kg °C, how much heat is recoverable per hour, using Q = V × ρ × Cp × ΔT?","back":"About 4,31,827 kcal/h.","explain":"This is the book's worked example of the heat content formula. Only the temperature drop is recoverable, and Cp is per kg, so the volume flow must first be converted to a mass flow.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p247","steps":[{"prompt":"Write the formula and list the values.","answer":"Q = V × ρ × Cp × ΔT, with V = 2,100 m³/h, ρ = 1.19 kg/m³, Cp = 0.24 kcal/kg °C.","explain":"Each term converts the volume flow into a heat quantity per hour."},{"prompt":"Find the temperature difference.","answer":"ΔT = 900 − 180 = 720 °C.","explain":"Only the drop from inlet to exit temperature can be recovered."},{"prompt":"Find the mass flow of flue gas.","answer":"2,100 × 1.19 = 2,499 kg/h.","explain":"Cp is given per kg, so the flow must be in kg/h."},{"prompt":"Multiply mass flow × Cp × ΔT.","answer":"2,499 × 0.24 × 720 = 4,31,827 kcal/h.","explain":"This is the heat available per hour for recovery."}],"order":2383},
{"id":"p2-b08-006","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"problem","front":"Using the book's rule of thumb, what fuel saving comes from a recuperator that cuts flue gas from 900 °C to 180 °C?","back":"About 33% (720 ÷ 22 ≈ 32.7, quoted as 33%).","explain":"The book uses the rule of 1% fuel reduction for every 22 °C reduction in flue gas temperature. This is a rule-of-thumb estimate, not a design calculation.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p248","steps":[{"prompt":"State the rule.","answer":"1% fuel reduction for every 22 °C reduction in flue gas temperature.","explain":"This is the basis of the book's furnace example."},{"prompt":"Find the temperature drop.","answer":"900 − 180 = 720 °C.","explain":"The drop is the inlet temperature minus the final exhaust temperature."},{"prompt":"Divide the drop by 22.","answer":"720 ÷ 22 = 32.7 %.","explain":"Each 22 °C of drop gives 1% of fuel."},{"prompt":"Round as the book does.","answer":"About 33% fuel saving.","explain":"The book states the result as 33%."}],"order":2384},
{"id":"p2-b08-007","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"For a waste heat source that is a vapour stream, what extra heat becomes recoverable?","back":"When the vapour is condensed, its latent heat is also recoverable, in addition to its sensible heat.","explain":"Latent heat is released at a constant temperature during condensation. For steam or vapour streams it can be a large share of the total heat content.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p248","order":2385},
{"id":"p2-b08-008","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"When is heat lost in providing chilled water a high-grade waste heat source?","back":"High grade if it can be used to reduce the demand for refrigeration. Low grade if the refrigeration unit is used as a form of heat pump.","explain":"The grade depends on whether the recovered heat reduces refrigeration load or only provides low-temperature heat. The book classes this source in Table 8.1.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p248","order":2386},
{"id":"p2-b08-009","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"What are the typical waste gas temperature ranges of a glass melting furnace and of a dry-process cement kiln?","back":"Glass melting furnace: 1000–1550 °C. Dry-process cement kiln: 620–730 °C.","explain":"Both are high-temperature sources in the book's Table 8.2. The high-temperature range is where heat recovery usually gives the most valuable heat.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p249","order":2387},
{"id":"p2-b08-010","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"What are the typical exhaust temperature ranges of steam boilers and gas turbines in the medium-temperature band?","back":"Steam boiler exhausts: 230–480 °C. Gas turbine exhausts: 370–540 °C.","explain":"The book's Table 8.3 lists these as medium-temperature sources. Recovery here usually means steam generation or air heating.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p249","order":2388},
{"id":"p2-b08-011","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"True or false: a turbocharged reciprocating engine has a higher exhaust temperature range than a non-turbocharged one?","back":"False. Turbocharged: 230–370 °C. Non-turbocharged: 315–600 °C.","explain":"The book's Table 8.3 gives both ranges, and the turbocharged range is the lower one. Check these values against the table when answering trap questions.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p249","order":2389},
{"id":"p2-b08-012","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"What are the two categories of benefit from waste heat recovery?","back":"Direct benefits: lower utility consumption and cost, and lower process cost, because process efficiency improves. Indirect benefits: reduced pollution, smaller equipment sizes, and lower auxiliary energy use.","explain":"Direct benefits show up in the energy and process cost lines. Indirect benefits come from a smaller flue gas flow, which allows smaller fans, pumps and other equipment.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p250","order":2390},
{"id":"p2-b08-013","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"Which combustible wastes can be burnt in incinerators to recover heat and reduce pollution?","back":"Carbon monoxide gas, sour gas, carbon black off-gases, oil sludge, acrylonitrile and other plastic chemicals.","explain":"Burning these wastes in incinerators serves two purposes: the heat is recovered, and the release of toxic material to the atmosphere is reduced.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p250","order":2391},
{"id":"p2-b08-014","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"Which plant documents should be reviewed to understand a process before designing a waste heat recovery system?","back":"Process flow sheets, layout diagrams, piping isometrics, and electrical and instrumentation cable ducting drawings.","explain":"This review identifies sources and uses of waste heat, upset conditions caused by recovery, available space, and constraints such as dew point in equipment.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p251","order":2392},
{"id":"p2-b08-015","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"Name the economic parameters used to evaluate a waste heat recovery system.","back":"Investment, depreciation, payback period and rate of return. Advice from experienced consultants and suppliers should also be obtained.","explain":"The economic evaluation supports the choice of system. The book stresses that consultant and supplier advice is needed for a rational decision.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p251","order":2393},
{"id":"p2-b08-016","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"Why do recuperators often use parallel flow rather than the more efficient counterflow arrangement?","back":"Because the recuperator also cools the exhaust duct that carries the gases away, which extends that duct's service life.","explain":"Counterflow would transfer heat more efficiently. Parallel flow is kept because it keeps the duct wall cooler.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p252","order":2394},
{"id":"p2-b08-017","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"Why is the metallic concentric-tube recuperator called a 'radiation' recuperator?","back":"A substantial part of the heat passing from the hot gases to the inner tube is transferred by radiation. The cold air in the annulus is almost transparent to infrared, so it is heated only by convection.","explain":"The hot gas flows in the inner tube and the combustion air flows in the outer annulus. Only the radiant part of the transfer depends on the gas side being hot.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p252","order":2395},
{"id":"p2-b08-018","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"How do baffles change a tube-type (convective) recuperator?","back":"Baffles make the air pass over the hot tubes more than once: two baffles give a three-pass recuperator, and one baffle giving two passes is a two-pass design. Baffling raises effectiveness but also raises cost and the pressure drop in the combustion air path.","explain":"More passes give a larger effective heat transfer area and better effectiveness. The trade-off is extra cost and extra resistance to air flow.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p252","order":2396},
{"id":"p2-b08-019","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"Why are shell-and-tube recuperators more compact and more effective than radiation recuperators?","back":"They provide a larger heat transfer area through multiple tubes and multiple gas passes.","explain":"The book says shell and tube type recuperators are generally more compact and have higher effectiveness. This is why they are used where space and efficiency matter.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p252","order":2397},
{"id":"p2-b08-020","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"What temperature limits do ceramic recuperators allow on the gas side and on the preheated air side?","back":"Gas side up to 1550 °C; preheated air side up to 815 °C. Metal recuperator liners lose life at inlet temperatures above about 1100 °C.","explain":"Ceramic tube recuperators were developed to overcome the temperature limit of metal recuperators. Later designs use short silicon carbide tubes joined by flexible seals.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p253","order":2398},
{"id":"p2-b08-021","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"What happens to regenerator performance if the time between reversals is made very long?","back":"Thermal storage must be larger, which raises cost. The average preheat temperature is lower, which reduces fuel economy.","explain":"Regenerators are widely used in glass and steel melting furnaces for large capacities. The reversal period is a key design choice, balancing storage cost against preheat temperature.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p254","order":2399},
{"id":"p2-b08-022","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"What sensible heat transfer efficiency can a heat wheel reach, and how large have heat wheels been built?","back":"Up to 85%. Heat wheels have been built up to 21 m in diameter with air capacities up to 1130 m³/min.","explain":"A heat wheel is a rotating porous disk with high heat capacity that carries heat from the hot duct to the cold duct. It suits low to medium temperature recovery.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p255","order":2400},
{"id":"p2-b08-023","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"What are the three elements of a heat pipe?","back":"A sealed container, a capillary wick structure fused to the inner surface of the tube, and a working fluid.","explain":"The wick is sealed under vacuum with the working fluid. It returns the condensed liquid to the evaporator region.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p256","order":2401},
{"id":"p2-b08-024","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"How does a heat pipe move heat from the hot end to the cold end?","back":"At the hot end the working fluid evaporates and absorbs latent heat. The vapour travels to the cool end, condenses and gives up its latent heat. The liquid then flows back through the wick.","explain":"The heat pipe has no moving parts and needs no input power. Heat transfer depends on repeated evaporation and condensation of the working fluid.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p256","order":2402},
{"id":"p2-b08-025","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"What operating temperature and heat recovery capability do heat pipe heat exchangers offer?","back":"They can operate at 315 °C with 60% to 80% heat recovery.","explain":"The book also notes that heat pipe systems are lightweight and compact. They lower fan horsepower and need no cooling water or lubrication systems.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p256","order":2403},
{"id":"p2-b08-026","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"A heat pipe can transfer up to how many times more thermal energy than copper, the best known conductor?","back":"Up to 100 times.","explain":"Heat pipes absorb and transfer thermal energy with no moving parts, so they need minimal maintenance.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p255","order":2404},
{"id":"p2-b08-027","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"By how much does each 22 °C reduction in flue gas temperature reduce boiler fuel use?","back":"About 1% of the boiler's fuel.","explain":"This applies whether the heat goes to an economiser (feed water) or an air preheater (combustion air). The book uses it as the standard rule of thumb for boiler heat recovery.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p257","order":2405},
{"id":"p2-b08-028","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"How much feed water temperature rise in an economiser, and how much combustion air temperature rise in an air preheater, gives 1% boiler fuel saving?","back":"6 °C rise in feed water temperature, or 20 °C rise in combustion air temperature, gives 1% fuel saving.","explain":"These are the book's conversion figures for boiler heat recovery. They make it easy to estimate the saving from a measured temperature change.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p257","order":2406},
{"id":"p2-b08-029","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"When must a shell-and-tube heat exchanger be used for waste heat recovery?","back":"When the waste heat medium is a liquid or vapour that heats another liquid, because both paths must be sealed to contain their pressures. A condensing vapour is normally kept in the shell.","explain":"The higher-pressure fluid is circulated in the tubes, since the shell is inherently weaker. Vapour condensing inside small parallel tubes causes flow instability, so it is placed in the shell.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p258","order":2407},
{"id":"p2-b08-030","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"Why is a plate heat exchanger used when the temperature difference is small?","back":"When temperature differences are small, the cost of heat exchange surface is a major factor. Thin corrugated plates separated by gaskets give a large surface in a compact unit. Typical uses are milk pasteurisation and food evaporation.","explain":"The hot and cold streams flow in alternate channels between the plates. The corrugations improve heat transfer.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p258","order":2408},
{"id":"p2-b08-031","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"What is a waste heat boiler, and how does it produce steam?","back":"It is usually a water-tube boiler in which hot exhaust gases from gas turbines, incinerators and similar sources pass over water-filled tubes. The water is vaporised in the tubes and the steam is collected in a steam drum.","explain":"Because the exhaust gases are usually in the medium-temperature range, the water tubes are often finned to increase the gas-side heat transfer area and make the boiler compact.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p259","order":2409},
{"id":"p2-b08-032","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"What is the rule-of-thumb temperature limit for waste heat recovery, and why?","back":"Below about 120 °C (or better, 150 °C, for a safe margin) is usually the limit, because of the risk of condensation of corrosive liquids.","explain":"Rising fuel costs can make even low-temperature heat worth using for space heating. The corrosion risk sets the practical floor for recovery.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p261","order":2410},
{"id":"p2-b08-033","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"Describe the four steps of the vapour compression heat pump cycle.","back":"1) The evaporator extracts heat from the waste heat source and boils the working fluid. 2) The compressor raises its pressure and temperature, using external work. 3) The condenser delivers heat to the user. 4) The throttling valve reduces the pressure back to the evaporator condition, and the cycle repeats.","explain":"It is a closed cycle: the circulating substance is kept separate from the source and user streams and reused each time round.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p261","order":2411},
{"id":"p2-b08-034","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"How much heat can a heat pump upgrade, relative to the energy the device consumes?","back":"More than twice the energy consumed by the device.","explain":"A heat pump lifts low-grade heat from ambient air, water or earth to a useful temperature by doing compression work with an electric motor-driven compressor.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p262","order":2412},
{"id":"p2-b08-035","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"Why are heat pump applications most promising when both heating and cooling are used?","back":"Example: in a plastics factory, chilled water from the heat pump cools the injection moulding machines, while the heat output warms the factory or office.","explain":"Using both outputs makes the heat pump's cost go further. The book also lists product drying and maintaining dry storage atmospheres as applications.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p262","order":2413},
{"id":"p2-b08-036","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"fact","front":"What is a direct contact heat exchanger, and where is it typically used?","back":"Low pressure steam is condensed directly in incoming water, which is sprayed over trays or packed beds, heating it. The typical application is the deaerator of a steam generating station.","explain":"Direct contact works only where the steam and water are miscible, as the book notes. The steam is completely condensed in the incoming water.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p262","order":2414},
{"id":"p2-b08-037","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"problem","front":"A crude oil furnace heats 500 m³/h of crude (SG 0.85, Cp 0.6 kcal/kg °C) from 255 °C to 360 °C, firing 3.4 t/h of fuel oil with GCV 9,850 kcal/kg. An air preheater heats 57 t/h of combustion air (Cp 0.24) from 28 °C to 195 °C. What is furnace efficiency before and after the air preheater?","back":"Before: 80%. After: 85.8%.","explain":"The air preheater returns heat to the furnace, so less fuel is needed for the same heat gain in the crude. The heat gain by the crude does not change; only the heat input falls.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p263","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the heat gain by the crude.","answer":"500 × 1000 × 0.85 × 0.6 × (360 − 255) = 26,775,000 kcal/h.","explain":"Volume (m³) × 1000 × SG gives mass in kg/h."},{"prompt":"Find the heat input before the air preheater.","answer":"3.4 × 1000 × 9,850 = 33,490,000 kcal/h.","explain":"Fuel in t/h is converted to kg/h and multiplied by GCV."},{"prompt":"Find the furnace efficiency before the air preheater.","answer":"26,775,000 ÷ 33,490,000 = 80%.","explain":"Efficiency is heat gain divided by heat input."},{"prompt":"Find the heat recovered by the air preheater.","answer":"57 × 1000 × 0.24 × (195 − 28) = 2,284,560 kcal/h.","explain":"Air is heated from the 28 °C ambient, not from zero."},{"prompt":"Find the new heat input and the efficiency after the air preheater.","answer":"33,490,000 − 2,284,560 = 31,205,440 kcal/h; efficiency = 26,775,000 ÷ 31,205,440 = 85.8%.","explain":"The heat saved from the flue gas reduces the fuel input for the same heat gain."}],"order":2415},
{"id":"p2-b08-038","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"problem","front":"A boiler economiser cuts flue gas temperature from 260 °C to 150 °C. Using the book's rule, what is the fuel saving?","back":"About 5% of boiler fuel.","explain":"The rule is 1% fuel saving per 22 °C drop in flue gas temperature. The 1,000 kg/h fuel figure in the explanation is only an example to show scale.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p257","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the flue gas temperature drop.","answer":"260 − 150 = 110 °C.","explain":"The drop is the gas temperature entering minus the gas temperature leaving."},{"prompt":"State the rule.","answer":"1% boiler fuel saving per 22 °C drop in flue gas temperature.","explain":"This is the book's rule for economisers and air preheaters."},{"prompt":"Divide the drop by 22.","answer":"110 ÷ 22 = 5.","explain":"Five steps of 22 °C give five percent."},{"prompt":"Convert to fuel quantity (example).","answer":"Result: 5% of boiler fuel; for example, 5% of 1,000 kg/h = 50 kg/h.","explain":"Rule-of-thumb estimate only; the actual saving depends on the boiler and the operating conditions."}],"order":2416},
{"id":"p2-b08-039","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"problem","front":"A hospital cooling plant recovers 28,225 kcal/h of heat from exhaust air and its capacity falls by 9.33 TR. If plant capacity costs Rs 12,000 per TR, what is the capital cost saving?","back":"About Rs 1,12,000 (9.33 × Rs 12,000 = Rs 1,11,960).","explain":"This is the book's case example, which states the saving as Rs 1,12,000. The chapter does not date the cost figures, so the year is not known from the text.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p257","steps":[{"prompt":"Read the given values.","answer":"Plant capacity reduction = 9.33 TR; capital cost = Rs 12,000 per TR.","explain":"The 28,225 kcal/h recovered heat gives the 9.33 TR reduction in the book."},{"prompt":"Multiply capacity reduction by capital cost.","answer":"9.33 × 12,000 = Rs 1,11,960.","explain":"This is the capital cost avoided by not installing the extra capacity."},{"prompt":"Round as the book does.","answer":"About Rs 1,12,000.","explain":"The book states Rs 1,12,000 as the capital cost saving."},{"prompt":"Keep the operating cost separate.","answer":"Operating electricity cost is Rs 268 per million kcal (based on 0.8 kW/TR) in the book.","explain":"Capital saving and operating cost are separate figures and must not be added together without the operating data."}],"order":2417},
{"id":"p2-b08-040","paper":2,"topic":"Waste Heat Recovery","kind":"problem","front":"A carbon black plant vents CO-rich gas. An incinerator with a waste heat boiler and steam turbine costs about Rs 350 lakh and saves about Rs 160 lakh a year in power. What is the simple payback period?","back":"About 2.2 years (350 ÷ 160 = 2.19).","explain":"The book does not state a payback for this case. This simple payback is derived from its cost and saving figures and ignores interest, running costs and the pollution benefit.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 2 Ch 08 p260","steps":[{"prompt":"Identify the capital cost and the annual saving.","answer":"Capital cost Rs 350 lakh; annual saving about Rs 160 lakh per annum.","explain":"Both figures are given in the case example."},{"prompt":"Divide the cost by the annual saving.","answer":"350 ÷ 160 = 2.19 years.","explain":"Simple payback is capital cost divided by annual net saving."},{"prompt":"Round the answer.","answer":"About 2.2 years.","explain":"The saving is an approximate figure (tilde in the book), so the answer is approximate too."},{"prompt":"Check what the estimate leaves out.","answer":"Simple payback ignores the time value of money and the running costs of the new equipment.","explain":"Treat the result as a first estimate, as the book suggests for investment decisions."}],"order":2418}
]$cards$::jsonb);
